                "nodes": {
                  "q1": "base",
                  "q0": "active"
                },
                "note": "…and again"
              },
              {
                "at": "bottom",
                "nodes": {
                  "A": "dim",
                  "q0": "base"
                },
                "note": "CPU-bound jobs sink to the bottom"
              },
              {
                "at": "interactive",
                "show": [
                  "B"
                ],
                "nodes": {
                  "B": "active",
                  "q2": "active"
                },
                "note": "B: a short burst, then waits for input"
              },
              {
                "at": "responds",
                "nodes": {
                  "B": "done"
                },
                "note": "B stays on top: fast response"
              },
              {
                "at": "lengths",
                "nodes": {
                  "A": "base",
                  "q2": "base"
                },
                "note": "≈ SJF, with no job lengths"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "Two loopholes. Interactive jobs can starve A at the bottom forever, and a job that turns interactive never climbs back. So every so often, a priority boost moves every job to the top. And a job could game the scheduler, giving up the CPU just before its slice ends, to stay on top. So the allotment counts all the CPU time used at a level, however often the job gives the CPU up. The number of queues, the slices, and the boost period all need tuning. BSD, Solaris and Windows all use variants of this design.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Closing the <em>loopholes</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "starve",
                "label": "Starvation",
                "title": "Priority boost",
                "body": "Every period S, move all jobs to the top.",
                "code": "Rule 5"
              },
              {
                "at": "game",
                "label": "Gaming",
                "title": "Count all CPU time",
                "body": "The allotment covers all time used at a level.",
                "code": "Rule 4, revised"
              },
              {
                "at": "tuning",
                "label": "Tuning",
                "title": "Voodoo constants",
