              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "Weights come from nice values. Each nice step is worth about ten percent of CPU, a factor of one point two five in weight, and a heavier task's virtual runtime grows more slowly. A task that slept, waiting for input, has fallen behind, so it runs soon after it wakes. That was the completely fair scheduler. Since Linux six point six, the default is E E V D F. Each task has a lag: the CPU time it's owed. Only tasks with lag at least zero are eligible, and among those, the earliest virtual deadline runs first. A task asking for shorter slices gets earlier deadlines, so it runs sooner, without getting more CPU overall.",
          "props": {
