    {
      "id": "04",
      "title": "Many CPUs",
      "question": "With many CPUs, should there be one shared run queue, or one per CPU?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question four. On a machine with many CPUs, should runnable tasks wait in one shared queue, or in one queue per CPU? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "One run queue, or <em>one per CPU</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "One shared queue is simple, and balances load perfectly. But every CPU takes the same lock to pick a task, and that lock becomes the bottleneck. And tasks hop between CPUs, losing their warm caches. One queue per CPU scales: each CPU mostly touches its own queue, and tasks keep their cache affinity. The cost is imbalance: one CPU can sit idle while another has a backlog.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Shared or <em>per-CPU</em>",
            "left": {
              "at": "shared",
              "label": "Single queue",
              "title": "Simple, balanced",
              "body": "One lock for all CPUs; tasks bounce and lose warm caches."
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "scales",
              "label": "Per-CPU queues",
              "title": "Scales, keeps affinity",
              "tone": "good",
              "body": "Each CPU uses its own queue; caches stay warm."
            },
            "verdict": {
              "at": "cost",
              "text": "The catch: load imbalance.",
              "code": "idle CPU, busy neighbour"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "So per CPU queues need balancing. An idle CPU steals work from a busy one, and Linux also rebalances periodically, preferring nearby CPUs that share a cache, before crossing sockets. Migrating a task costs its warm cache, so the balancer is deliberately lazy. And when latency matters most, you pin: set the thread's affinity to one core, and keep other work off that core.",
          "props": {
